  "worked_solution": "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\u2207f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f & =\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}\\,i+\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}\\,j+\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$",
